Z||000008||StuGood||07-24-2000||05:20 PM||||Well, slap me now and get it over with  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">.<BR>I <B>always</B> get myself into trouble when I generalize  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">. <P>I <B>shouldn't</B> have said, "GM Sychromesh Fluid in everything but automatics," because I really don't know anything about the Isuzu transaxles.  As a matter of fact, I'm getting an education here - didn't know Isuzu made a 4-speed for the Fiero.          <P>So please further my education. Penetrator, am I correct that your Fiero is a 1986 model?  What engine does yours have - 2.5l?||||reg||4
Z||000009||penetrator||07-25-2000||05:26 AM||||Stugood:<P>Im not 100% sure that mine is an Izusu. But it just looks like that in the Chiltons manual. And im not sure if that one is printed correctly. No it is a 86 fiero V6 2.8l with *.* 4-speed manual.  =)<P>i got pictures of it at <A HREF="http://www.hyperwerks.se/fiero" TARGET=_blank>www.hyperwerks.se/fiero</A>  <P>Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000010||Gold-86SE||07-25-2000||10:25 AM||||<BR>Forgive my ignorance, but will the rpo code on the left front fender give you a indication of the tranny installed in the Fiero.<P>Like M19 = 4.10<P>If we can figure this out, we can probably determine type of trans.<P>Mine has the M17, which is 3.65 gearing, and I use 5w30 oil, and have had no problems. Sorry I am not sure of the manufacturer.<P>Hope this helps.<BR><P>------------------<BR>Gold-86SE (Fiero Collector)||||reg||1
Z||000011||fiero56||07-25-2000||11:45 AM||||<P>'84 4-cyl= 4-speed<BR>'85-'88 4-cyl= Izuzu 5-speed<BR>'85-'86 6-cyl= Muncie 4-speed<BR>'86-'88 6-cyl= Getrag 5-speed||||reg||1
Z||000012||2birds||07-25-2000||07:18 PM||||I've gone 166,000 miles with Havoline Formula 3 5W-30 in my Getrag.  I shifted to Havoline Synthetic because I had some 5W-30 left over from my Super Coupe T-bird, and I run 10W-30 in my 4.6 'bird.  The synthetic doesn't seem to be any different to me, but they seem to be the rage for the Mustang crowd to put in their T-5's, which I believe used ATF originally anyway.  I usually change mine every year.  It's only 2.75 quarts.<BR>So where did the advice to use GM synchromesh fluid come from?  I'm a bit leery of change for change sake, after all the grief I saw about using ATF in clutch hydraulics.  ||||reg||1
Z||000013||Standard||07-25-2000||11:34 PM||||The advice to use GM Syncromesh fluid came straight from GM.  <P>5-speed Getrag: GM initially recommended 5W-30 engine oil; sometime in 1988 they changed the recommendation to GM Synchromesh Fluid, P/N 12345349. <P>For more info check out this page: <A HREF="http://members.xoom.com/fierov6/OSG/trans-fluid.html" TARGET=_blank>http://members.xoom.com/fierov6/OSG/trans-fluid.html</A> <P>------------------<BR>Mike -- Golden Valley, MN<BR>Black 86.5 GT 5 Spd, 147,000 miles and counting!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/BURNOUT.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000014||StuGood||07-26-2000||12:50 PM||||Penetrator, I looked at your website pictures, and have determined 2 things:<P>1) The transaxle in the photos <B>looks</B> a lot like the one in my 1986 SE (original equipment V6 + 4-speed), which is a 4-speed Muncie.  Of course, I 've been wrong a lot lately  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"><P>2) I'm so jealous of your vehicles, I absolutely refuse to help you anymore (just kidding)  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> ! Great pictures!<P>But, as someone posted earlier, check out the label in the front compartment for the transmission code.  If it's the original transmission, that should peg it for sure.<P>     ||||reg||1
Z||000015||Phantom Rage||07-27-2000||07:44 PM||||Ok, I'm mad.  I changed my 88 Isuzu 5-speed fluid with 5w30 Mobile 1 Synthetic engine oil. The good news is that the old fluid looked a little bit dirty.  The bad news is that the new oil made my shifter feel notchier.  <P>I had Pep Boys put in my clutch, pressure plate, and throw out bearing, when I first purchased the car.  That was about 2 years ago.  I called them today to ask what fluid they put in.  The service manager said 5w30 is what they would have put in.  I'm not to sure though, since my shifter was much smoother when they did it.<P>An employee at Autozone told me I need something with a GL-4 rating.  Ok, I'm not a rocket scientist but 75w90-gear oil seems to be way more viscous than 5w30 engine oil.  Does the oil viscosity account for smother shifting?<P>What is the correct fluid for an 88 Isuzu 5-speed? <P>Please, someone help.  I don’t think I’m gonna drive my yellow banana much until I have the right stuff in her <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><BR>||||reg||5
Z||000016||Indiana_resto_guy||07-27-2000||08:25 PM||||1) All 4 cylinders all years all 4 speeds-MM4 Isuzu. Use either syco fluid or 5w30.<P>2) The V-6 cars with 4 speeds-MM4 same as above.<P>3) 85-87 4 cylinders 5 speeds MM5 Isuzu. Same lubricant as above.<P>4) V-6 cars with 5 speeds MT5 Muncie (Getrag)<BR>Same lubricant as above.<P>NEVER USE 70-90 OR 80-90 GEAR LUBE!<p>[This message has been edited by Indiana_resto_guy (edited 07-27-2000).]||||reg||4
Z||000017||2birds||07-27-2000||09:58 PM||||Standard,<P>Thanks for the link.  I forgot all about it, and it's one of my favorites.||||reg||1
Z||000018||penetrator||07-28-2000||07:08 AM||||Stugood:<BR>Okey, now i finally knows witch trans i got. <P>And im gonna try with 5W-30 oil and see how its gonna behave. =)<P><BR>thanks||||reg||1
